  "content": {
    "body": "A **rule** pebble is a set of prescriptive CONSTRAINTS — the norms that MUST\n/ SHOULD / MAY be followed when implementing or verifying. It is a\nCHECKLIST pebble whose ITEMS are the individual rule statements, each with\nan ENFORCEMENT level. Rules are DOCTRINE, not work items: a violation\nlives on the violating unit and in a finding, NEVER on the rule itself.\nThis doc DEFINES every component and WHAT belongs under it; skills / agents\n/ knowledge REFERENCE it and own the METHOD.\n\nRead each entry as: the component, then the values/shape considered part\nof it.\n\n## How rules are used (the reference direction)\n\nRules are the constraints an implementer must hold to. They are REFERENCED\nBY ONLY the two doctrine-carrier types — nothing else:\n\n- **agents** — a persona references the rules it must uphold.\n- **skills** — a procedure references the rules that gate its output.\n\nA rule points only DOWN, to **knowledge** — the standard/background it is\ngrounded in. So the reference graph is: agents / skills → rule → knowledge.\nA rule is NEVER referenced by a model / structure / finding / workflow /\nknowledge / memory (those don't \"follow\" rules — the agent implementing\nthem does); ONLY skills and agents may reference a rule. And a rule\nreferences ONLY knowledge — nothing else.\n\n## Pebble-level components\n\n- **name** — the ruleset's name (\"Backend conventions\", \"Security rules\").\n- **status** — the rule's LIFECYCLE (not a work-item status): Draft\n  (`planned`) → Active (`verified`) → Inactive (`deprecated`). A rule is\n  written and activated, not \"built\"; violations never change its status.\n- **description** — what the ruleset governs and when it applies.\n- **references** — a rule references ONLY **knowledge** (the doc/standard\n  it is grounded in). No models, structures, findings, skills, workflows,\n  or other rules.\n- **resources** — NONE. A rule describes the AI's constraints, not the\n  codebase, so it carries no code links (the CLI refuses `link-code` on a\n  rule). The same holds for agents and skills; knowledge MAY carry\n  resources (it often describes an architecture or subsystem).\n\n## Rule-item components — the categories under each rule statement\n\nA rule's content is a checklist; each item is ONE rule statement.\n\n- **text** — the rule statement itself, imperative and single-norm\n  (\"Actions are single-purpose\", \"Never log secrets or PII\"). One norm per\n  item.\n- **enforcement** — the level this norm carries. Under it: ONE of —\n  - **strict** — MUST. Non-negotiable; a violation marks the violating unit\n    BROKEN.\n  - **recommended** — SHOULD. A violation surfaces as DRIFT / a warning, not\n    a hard block.\n  - **optional** — MAY. Advisory; note it, do not block.\n  (Enforcement is the rule item's meta — it takes the place of a checklist\n  item's priority/status.)\n- **description** — the detail behind the statement: the rationale, the\n  how, the edge cases and exceptions.\n- **refs** — links to the **knowledge** that grounds THIS statement\n  (knowledge only, same as the pebble level).\n\n## Status — Draft / Active / Inactive\n\nRules are doctrine, not tasks, so they use only three lifecycle states:\nDraft (`planned`) = being written, not in force · Active (`verified`) = in\nforce · Inactive (`deprecated`) = retired. Whether the code OBEYS a rule is\nrecorded on the violating unit (drift / broken) and in findings — never on\nthe rule.\n\n## Quick map — component → what's under it\n\n| Component | Under it (shape / allowed values) |\n|---|---|\n| pebble.name | ruleset name (string) |\n| pebble.status | Draft (planned) · Active (verified) · Inactive (deprecated) |\n| pebble.description | what the ruleset governs + when it applies |\n| references | knowledge ONLY (the grounding) |\n| item.text | the rule statement (imperative, one norm) |\n| item.enforcement | strict (MUST) · recommended (SHOULD) · optional (MAY) |\n| item.description | rationale / how / edge cases |\n| item.refs | knowledge that grounds this statement |\n| referenced BY | agents · skills ONLY (no other type) |\n\n## Rule CLI — all rule operations live here\n\nA rule's content kind is **checklist**; each item is a rule statement whose\nmeta is its **enforcement**. The write is HARDENED: enforcement, when\npresent on any item, must be strict / recommended / optional.\n\nCREATE a ruleset with its statements (enforcement per item):\n\n    pebbles-cli create rules --title \"Backend conventions\" --content '{\n      \"kind\": \"checklist\",\n      \"items\": [\n        { \"text\": \"Actions are single-purpose — one public handle() method\", \"enforcement\": \"strict\", \"description\": \"Split multi-step logic into composed Actions.\" },\n        { \"text\": \"Validate writes with a Form Request\", \"enforcement\": \"recommended\", \"description\": \"Keep controllers thin; centralize rules.\" },\n        { \"text\": \"Prefer named routes over string paths\", \"enforcement\": \"optional\", \"description\": \"Advisory — improves refactor safety.\" }\n      ]\n    }'\n\nAPPEND a statement (add-item carries --enforcement for rules):\n\n    pebbles-cli add-item rules/backend-conventions.pebble --text \"Never log secrets or PII\" --enforcement strict --description \"Redact tokens, passwords and card numbers before logging\"\n\nGROUND a statement in knowledge — link it from Properties → References, or\nset the item's refs via update; the target is a knowledge pebble only.\n\nSTATUS — the lifecycle (pebble-level; a --unit targets one statement):\n\n    pebbles-cli set-status rules/backend-conventions.pebble verified      # Active\n    pebbles-cli set-status rules/backend-conventions.pebble planned        # Draft\n    pebbles-cli set-status rules/backend-conventions.pebble deprecated      # Inactive (retire)\n\nINSPECT — connections both ways (what grounds it; who references it):\n\n    pebbles-cli refs rules/backend-conventions.pebble\n    pebbles-cli get rules/backend-conventions.pebble\n    pebbles-cli validate rules/backend-conventions.pebble\n\nThere is no delete — retire a rule with status `deprecated`.\n\n## Method lives elsewhere\n\nThis doc DEFINES the components. HOW rules are authored, applied during\nimplementation, and checked during verification lives in the skills /\nagents that reference them and in the **spec-lifecycle** / **verification-\nmodel** docs.",
    "kind": "text"
  },
